.TH CCOL_SET_THREAD_NAME 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_set_thread_name \- rename the calling thread and make its log records carry the new name
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_set_thread_name(const char *thread_name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Renames the calling thread, with prctl(PR_SET_NAME) on Linux,
pthread_setname_np(3) on FreeBSD and macOS, and pthread_set_name_np(3)
on OpenBSD.  The next record that the thread writes, through any logger,
carries the new name in the thread part of its proc field.

Every record names the process and the thread that wrote it.  A thread
reads its process ID, thread ID and thread name once, the first time it
logs, and keeps them for the rest of its life, so a record costs no
system call for them.  This function updates the name that the thread
keeps.  A child that fork(2) creates reads all three again.

The Linux kernel stores at most 15 bytes of a thread name, so the
function keeps the first 15 bytes of thread_name on every platform, and
the records and tools such as ps(1) and top(1) show the same name.

No logger is needed, so a thread can name itself before any logger
exists.  Only the calling thread is affected.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
thread_name  The new name.  It must not be NULL or empty.  Only its
             first 15 bytes are used.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        The thread has the new name, and its records
                    carry it.
ccol_invalid_args   thread_name is NULL or empty.  Nothing changes.
ccol_not_permitted  The system refused the rename, or the platform is
                    not Linux, FreeBSD, OpenBSD or macOS.  Nothing
                    changes.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
If a thread is renamed by any other means after its first record, for
example with pthread_setname_np(3) or prctl(PR_SET_NAME), its records
keep the old name, so rename a thread that logs with this function.
Thread-safe.  Each thread renames only itself.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
static void *worker(void *arg) {
    clog lg = *(clog *)arg;
    if (ccol_set_thread_name("ingest-worker") != ccol_success)
        return NULL;
    clog_info(lg, "started");
    /* proc=myapp(1234):ingest-worker(1240) */
    return NULL;
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_create_from_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_create_from_file_mp (3),
